{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"20e3d375e3767c5a0ea5acc4329d0238b08ebe76c7d8c8e6c5a1df44d79a427f","slug":"text-classification","location":"/chapters/appD_transformers/3_text_classification.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Text Classification","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"3_text_classification.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/3_text_classificatio-1c877233fb56f84ad8bc7c1bd45982ed.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","kind":"seealso","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where this is used","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"w3y2cBcDgu"}],"key":"zgfmUMnjBb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intent Routing and Classification","key":"Z6c8r6ZMmH"}],"identifier":"advanced-text-processing-intent-routing","label":"advanced-text-processing-intent-routing","url":"/intent-routing-and-classification","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/intent-routing-and-classification.json","key":"aFcqL3Gu1s"},{"type":"text","value":" routes queries with Naive Bayes classifiers over words and character n-grams. ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"VEabrkKz4M"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automated Feature Extraction","key":"j5HwYqdvAD"}],"identifier":"content-analysis-feature-extraction","label":"content-analysis-feature-extraction","url":"/automated-feature-extraction","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/automated-feature-extraction.json","key":"Am7VvrAN48"},{"type":"text","value":" treats text labels such as sentiment, topic, and language as the cheapest structural metadata, and ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"HC4z01Doj9"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multimodal Transformers","key":"Wje76FClRf"}],"identifier":"content-analysis-transformers","label":"content-analysis-transformers","url":"/multimodal-transformers","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/multimodal-transformers.json","key":"OFpwxyGaG5"},{"type":"text","value":" shows that the same JSON prompt that describes an image can classify a text. ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"wqmSxejISV"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evaluating Text Classifiers","key":"NLhlCzwXR9"}],"identifier":"performance-evaluation-text-classifiers","label":"performance-evaluation-text-classifiers","url":"/evaluating-text-classifiers","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/evaluating-text-classifiers.json","key":"YsrHyLilpf"},{"type":"text","value":" evaluates text classifiers, and ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"H45nFuKoD3"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When Evaluation Misleads","key":"mTOYWigNLb"}],"identifier":"performance-evaluation-limits","label":"performance-evaluation-limits","url":"/when-evaluation-misleads","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/when-evaluation-misleads.json","key":"AwbzqRAbSB"},{"type":"text","value":" warns about benchmarks that measure something other than the task. This section compares the classifiers on one dataset.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"AdTr8wQweH"}],"key":"dCHJgJy04l"}],"key":"qPI9bD3dkd"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text classification assigns a label to a text: a sentiment to a product review, a topic to a news article, an intent to a query, a language to a document. The labels feed facets and filters, route requests, and summarize large collections, for example the share of negative comments under a video. This section follows one task, the sentiment of tweets, through four generations of classifiers: Naive Bayes, a convolutional network, a transformer with a classification head, and zero-shot classification by embeddings and prompts. On the way, it shows that the most important step comes before any classifier: checking what the labels mean.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"k0beoTxW5y"}],"key":"bNsakKsGDM"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sentiment Analysis","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"ukRcHHjxvm"}],"identifier":"sentiment-analysis","label":"Sentiment Analysis","html_id":"sentiment-analysis","implicit":true,"key":"SSu5BnXuli"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sentiment analysis","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"tueBZwmWWR"}],"key":"OyW5UNqfe3"},{"type":"text","value":" decides whether a text expresses a positive, negative, or neutral opinion; finer variants recognize emotions such as anger, fear, joy, or surprise. Companies use it to monitor reviews and social media, and platforms use it to moderate content. Many statements are easy, because a few words carry the sentiment: “I like this product” is positive, “The food was really bad” negative, “I was going to the town” neutral. Others are hard:","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"fU5VqdK8Cg"}],"key":"nvoSM4p9Dn"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"negation","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"VLltssuNjO"}],"key":"gIG6lFFKHE"},{"type":"text","value":": “I can’t say I liked it” contains the positive word “liked”;","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"YnhbXZSCR6"}],"key":"m6hFj8RP3t"}],"key":"CVoCOYxnjf"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"unclear polarity","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"vlvfCMT5PQ"}],"key":"HXaVkNfqy6"},{"type":"text","value":": “Drinking wine is not my thing” may be negative or merely a preference;","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"NcOyVucros"}],"key":"wStckMGY9F"}],"key":"WZmpQZML8p"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"sarcasm","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"XxJT5LBAH3"}],"key":"hjCrANKaMa"},{"type":"text","value":": “What a fine artist you’ve become!” may mean the opposite of its words;","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"mgt9RCIlxq"}],"key":"aErs7VIYHu"}],"key":"ONx5q29wVS"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"multiple negations","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"ym1CUYvB9S"}],"key":"dy2vtliKkW"},{"type":"text","value":": “I haven’t ever owed anything to anyone” is full of negative words but positive in meaning.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"ZDPWCGath0"}],"key":"qSSvlJF9Uv"}],"key":"ecos3G44xx"}],"key":"iNcXDxMpzw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Classifiers that treat a text as a bag of words cannot resolve these cases; models that read words in context can, at least in part.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"h4uwZdqbIc"}],"key":"JR11qWrb1c"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Naive Bayes Baseline","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"Y9VLnaW5Jg"}],"identifier":"a-naive-bayes-baseline","label":"A Naive Bayes Baseline","html_id":"a-naive-bayes-baseline","implicit":true,"key":"o1qibIrwRT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK) provides a sentiment dataset of 5,000 positive and 5,000 negative tweets. We split it into 8,000 training and 2,000 test tweets, stratified so that both classes keep their shares. A tweet tokenizer lowercases the text, removes user names and links, and shortens repeated characters such as “sooooo”. Since tweets are short and most words occur only once, each tweet becomes a ","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"ViU3opCj7u"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"set of words","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"AxEjjZ6qkC"}],"key":"TiLE2jzgr1"},{"type":"text","value":", and a Bernoulli Naive Bayes classifier (","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"A8wP8uxMoy"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Naive Bayes","key":"W2Cp102yji"}],"identifier":"appendix-naive-bayes","label":"appendix-naive-bayes","url":"/naive-bayes","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/naive-bayes.json","key":"Og8obpv3RN"},{"type":"text","value":") with Laplace smoothing learns from the presence and absence of each of the 11,241 distinct tokens.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"JGjstBaIAw"}],"key":"xEgHn1ccuS"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Naive Bayes on 10,000 tweets","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"jk4wlZvwlQ"}],"key":"A1CzSCfqfW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Training takes 64 milliseconds on a laptop, and classifying the 2,000 test tweets 13 milliseconds. The classifier is correct on 99.98% of the training tweets and on 99.85% of the test tweets: it makes 3 errors in 2,000. The most informative features for the positive class are “:)”, “:-)”, “:D”, and “:p”; for the negative class “:(”, “:-(”, and “>:(”.","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"bArbXPaX9s"}],"key":"HC0iYKwHXF"}],"key":"Eh70jdFxs8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Such an accuracy should make us suspicious. Sentiment is hard, and no classifier understands sarcasm from a bag of words. The informative features point to the explanation.","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"Cg5jutpBWN"}],"key":"LHAz9JYQom"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Look at the Labels","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"BgIcStc9DR"}],"identifier":"a-look-at-the-labels","label":"A Look at the Labels","html_id":"a-look-at-the-labels","implicit":true,"key":"Zauih6l8Np"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"According to the documentation of the dataset, the tweets were collected in July 2015 by searching for emoticons: the positive tweets with strings such as “:)”, “:D”, and “<3”, the negative tweets with strings such as “:(”, “:/”, and “>:(”. The labels were never assigned by people; they are the emoticons. In the data, 99.0% of the positive tweets contain a smiling emoticon and 98.1% of the negative tweets a frowning one. The classifier has learned the rule by which the dataset was collected, not the sentiment of the text.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"I2Kvs8hoSC"}],"key":"IyFi35XIVt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removing all emoticons from the tweets turns the task into the one we actually care about: deciding the sentiment from the words. The same Naive Bayes classifier then reaches 79.0% on the test tweets, with 420 errors instead of 3. Many tweets without their emoticon are routine messages with little sentiment: 62 of the positive tweets thank someone for following, such as “Thanks for following! Do get in touch if you’d like any more info about our project”. The remaining errors include the hard cases above.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"wFT0kFfw3Q"}],"key":"ba4tidbdU4"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"warning","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a feature gives away the label","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"LHVSanEsFX"}],"key":"gOJvXhQaPQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Labels derived automatically from a feature, here emoticons, are a cheap way to build large training sets, called distant supervision. They are also a trap: if the feature stays in the input, a classifier learns the labeling rule and reports an accuracy that new data, labeled by people, will never reach. Before trusting a high accuracy, check how the labels were made and which features the model relies on. This is a form of the leakage that ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"nkvtgkIELY"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Training and Model Selection","key":"ECSC8Wq57J"}],"identifier":"appendix-model-selection","label":"appendix-model-selection","url":"/training-and-model-selection","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/training-and-model-selection.json","key":"QE0ANHoQD9"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"g9AyWxbvmu"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When Evaluation Misleads","key":"olAM5iTVj4"}],"identifier":"performance-evaluation-limits","label":"performance-evaluation-limits","url":"/when-evaluation-misleads","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/when-evaluation-misleads.json","key":"bB6HFMJ0fQ"},{"type":"text","value":" describe.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"rSJxjEWsSK"}],"key":"AM3xjttyiG"}],"key":"nSgncrD7Ss"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"All results below use the tweets without emoticons, with the same split.","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"aM07DXMfpg"}],"key":"OyQeQUcIxH"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Neural Classifiers","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"PzTYRDWqt2"}],"identifier":"neural-classifiers","label":"Neural Classifiers","html_id":"neural-classifiers","implicit":true,"key":"jM9uPzbheR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"TextCNN","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"cnZ6YW1m4a"}],"key":"McSbSTVdxI"},{"type":"text","value":" (Kim 2014) was the first neural architecture to beat bag-of-words classifiers widely. It turns the tokens into word embeddings and slides one-dimensional convolutions of widths 2 to 5 over the sequence, each with several filters, so that every filter responds to a pattern of a few consecutive words, such as “not good”. Max pooling over the positions keeps the strongest response of each filter, which makes the output independent of the text length, and a fully connected layer with a softmax maps the pooled vector to the classes. TextCNN is a historical step: transformers replaced it for almost every task.","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"a6m3z5I0eh"}],"key":"qbt9Tl4Jy0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A transformer classifier puts the classification head of ","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"qFBcYpGLSi"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fine-Tuning","key":"dVEfjkxYYr"}],"identifier":"appendix-fine-tuning","label":"appendix-fine-tuning","url":"/fine-tuning","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/fine-tuning.json","key":"sOruyzeUwh"},{"type":"text","value":" on top of a pretrained encoder. We use MiniLM, a distilled encoder with 6 layers, 384 dimensions, and 22.7 million parameters, in three ways. A ","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"UXTPcNIBUr"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"frozen encoder","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"cKx7Kurj4O"}],"key":"n5dfncpr5e"},{"type":"text","value":" with a logistic regression on top of its sentence embeddings, a linear probe, needs only the embeddings, which take 30 seconds for all 10,000 tweets on a laptop processor; it reaches 79.1%, the same as Naive Bayes. ","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"zzVjl8bXCo"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fine-tuning","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"QNEWLGGhRH"}],"key":"Fr4zSGFgCk"},{"type":"text","value":" all weights for one epoch over the 8,000 training tweets with the loop of ","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"zYcc9jkqTp"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fine-Tuning","key":"Dymnl9yPzQ"}],"identifier":"appendix-fine-tuning","label":"appendix-fine-tuning","url":"/fine-tuning","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/fine-tuning.json","key":"r4sUyU4crI"},{"type":"text","value":" takes 189 seconds and reaches 82.8%, because the encoder can now adapt its representation to the sentiment of tweets.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"bKH6PC4zAK"}],"key":"q4lhbwPae5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A model ","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"AxARR6bVMV"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"fine-tuned by others","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"dJVkrZYEtX"}],"key":"ihUtsKKNYL"},{"type":"text","value":" can be used without any training. The RoBERTa model of the TweetEval benchmark was fine-tuned on tweets labeled by people with three classes, negative, neutral, and positive. Applied to our test tweets and forced to choose between positive and negative, it reaches 74.4%. It predicts “neutral” as its top label for 40% of the tweets without emoticons, an honest assessment for many of them, but our two labels do not allow it. A model from elsewhere brings its own definition of the classes.","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"AH0nX6EHRS"}],"key":"btiZYX7a4b"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zero-Shot Classification","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"key":"V4PwNriLWK"}],"identifier":"zero-shot-classification","label":"Zero-Shot Classification","html_id":"zero-shot-classification","implicit":true,"key":"PB1zZmCwUG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zero-shot classifiers need no labeled examples at all. The first approach uses ","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"H8uzt1mJ3t"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"label prototypes","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"Za25FbMV0d"}],"key":"qjP4Qz6Jcz"},{"type":"text","value":": we embed a short description of each class, “This tweet is positive and happy.” and “This tweet is negative and sad.”, and assign each tweet the class whose description is most similar to its embedding, as in the zero-shot classification of CLIP in ","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"eyMVCCsi51"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multimodal Transformers","key":"yOQBs8W0J9"}],"identifier":"content-analysis-transformers","label":"content-analysis-transformers","url":"/multimodal-transformers","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/multimodal-transformers.json","key":"SxdAWOPPtL"},{"type":"text","value":". With MiniLM, this reaches 67.5%: better than chance, but well below the trained classifiers. Adding a class only means adding a description.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"pDmXygmirg"}],"key":"Y6uV1b47xV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second approach uses a ","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"PJX0nCs7Lh"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"prompt","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"o6FvQAU7tS"}],"key":"uxZAndH2QG"},{"type":"text","value":" to a generative language model (","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"VBnjsYvTb8"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generative Language Models","key":"VpXHhw9LCY"}],"identifier":"appendix-generative-models","label":"appendix-generative-models","url":"/generative-language-models","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/generative-language-models.json","key":"WTjdUe9MJZ"},{"type":"text","value":"). The prompt describes the task and the classes and asks for the answer in a fixed format, typically JSON:","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"YzggOcKjLF"}],"key":"AgprE6ZEi8"},{"type":"code","lang":"text","value":"Classify the sentiment of the following tweet as \"positive\", \"negative\", or \"neutral\".\nAnswer only with JSON of the form {\"label\": \"...\", \"reason\": \"...\"}.\nTweet: \"Waiting at the airport for three hours now, just lovely.\"","position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"ijChzZsTyo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A capable model can recognize the sarcasm in this example, which no bag-of-words classifier can. Prompts handle new classes and new tasks immediately and can explain their decisions. Their costs are the opposite of Naive Bayes: every classification is a full generation, which takes seconds instead of microseconds and costs money with a hosted model, and the results vary with the wording of the prompt and between model versions. Confidence values that the model writes into its JSON are not calibrated probabilities (","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"key":"XNZ07xjzHE"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generative Language Models","key":"LZ0dYt6R8R"}],"identifier":"appendix-generative-models","label":"appendix-generative-models","url":"/generative-language-models","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/generative-language-models.json","key":"TE3fZXzdJF"},{"type":"text","value":").","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"key":"qgRyK1ki9N"}],"key":"KHy3COwkCo"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Two hosted models on the tweets","position":{"start":{"line":75,"column":1},"end":{"line":75,"column":1}},"key":"IvBN7Iq8g3"}],"key":"LVou0CZO5R"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We sent the prompt for each of the 2,000 test tweets without emoticons to Claude Haiku 5.5, a small and fast model, and to Claude Sonnet 5.5, a larger one, on Amazon Bedrock. With the three classes of the prompt above, the models answer “neutral” for 39% and 33% of the tweets, which the dataset counts as errors, because every tweet is labeled positive or negative. On the remaining tweets, they reach 81.1% and 83.3%. With a prompt that allows only “positive” or “negative”, Haiku reaches 75.1% and Sonnet 79.1%.","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"VPIvLH4v36"}],"key":"OquE96IxBg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two models agree with each other on 88% of the tweets, and on 17% both give the same answer against the label. Many of these tweets carried their sentiment only in the removed emoticon: “same” is labeled positive, “i hope so” negative. Of Sonnet’s errors, 48% are tweets it had called neutral when allowed to. The prompt matches Naive Bayes without a single labeled example, but not the fine-tuned encoder, which has learned from 8,000 examples which words come with which label. A model can only be as accurate as the labels allow, and these labels say how the author decorated the tweet, not what the text says.","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"kWtlKBdGGv"}],"key":"M8AoaeMRRS"}],"key":"xZadleAVG4"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trade-Offs","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"key":"MQMIg51rdh"}],"identifier":"trade-offs","label":"Trade-Offs","html_id":"trade-offs","implicit":true,"key":"YXToeBdY3z"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following table summarizes the methods on the 2,000 test tweets without emoticons. The local methods were measured on one laptop processor without a graphics processor. The prompts ran on Amazon Bedrock; their time per tweet was measured with one request at a time, and the costs follow from the token counts and the list prices.","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"key":"MRzJmB5BDN"}],"key":"O9DSNyzmPW"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Method","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"zRszNQhGx7"}],"key":"x5Rzn89KK